# The greeter compositor runs exactly one thing: the credential loop.
#
# NO HOME IS NAMED HERE ANYMORE: the auth store is system-side
# (/persist/veron-auth, read by the auth group), so the greeter
# verifies without knowing any user's home exists.
# ONE LOCK PER COMPOSITOR, NO SPIN. veron-lock exits 0 only when a
# person unlocked; it exits 2 on a compositor REFUSAL ("session already
# locked", session-lock.c:322) -- and retrying a refusal in a shell loop
# just asks the same wedged compositor again forever (machine #1,
# 2026-08-17: 90+ seconds of "session already locked" per boot). So a
# clean unlock passes; ANYTHING ELSE exits the greeter compositor, and
# the ROOT gate relaunches a whole fresh greeter (new labwc, new lock
# session) rather than a fresh client against a stale lock. The exit
# below drops us past pkill to end labwc; the gate's loop takes it from
# there. A wrong PASSWORD does not reach here -- veron-lock stays in its
# own event loop for re-entry and only returns on unlock or refusal.
if /usr/bin/veron-lock; then
    touch /run/greeter/passed
fi
# NOT $PPID: labwc reparents its autostart child, so $PPID was 1 and
# the old teardown printed "can't kill pid 1: Operation not permitted"
# (machine #1's log, 2026-08-16). pkill by name is safe here BECAUSE of
# the segregation: this shell is greeter, and greeter can only signal
# its own processes -- the one labwc it owns.
/bin/busybox pkill -x labwc
